From 4dca50ff0868740834637ad043c311d7162aac47 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:05:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] FEXCore: load FS and GS with MOV in 64-bit mode as Linux does

MOV to FS or GS in 64-bit mode was refused as undecodable, so the guest
got a different exception (Wine: illegal instruction) where hardware
loads the selector or raises #GP. It is now a data segment load against
Linux's x86-64 GDT: a null selector, or one of the four descriptors user
mode may use (32-bit and 64-bit user code, user data, CPU/node; base 0),
loads the selector and sets the base to 0; anything else (an LDT
selector without an LDT, kernel and system descriptors, empty TLS slots,
past the GDT limit) raises #GP with the selector as its error code,
delivered as SIGSEGV, si_code SI_KERNEL, trapno 13, selector and base
unchanged. Wine reports an LDT selector's #GP as an access violation with
information 0/0. MOV from FS or GS returns the selector last loaded, and
arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS/GS) leaves a null selector, as Linux does.

The #GP's error code is only known at run time: the new IR op
BreakErrorCode takes it from a register (Break with a constant error code
otherwise).

THE FINALS' and ARC Raiders' anti-cheat (Denuvo) do this: FEX's log for
THE FINALS shows "We don't support modifying FS selector in 64bit mode!"
115 times and the probe at the same address faulting every 20 s; ARC
Raiders' logs (StonkGuy/eac-arm64-emulation-poc#1, with a first patch by
the reporter that stored the selector without the checks) show the same.

eac-tests/seg-load64 (62 cases): native x86-64 PASS (oracle), FEX before
does not get past the first load, FEX after prints output identical to
native. ASM test Multiblock/InvalidBlockStateIsolation used MOV FS as its
unsupported instruction; it uses a32 stosb now.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
